                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "This is an perfectly okay aggressive stealth techno thriller.\n\nSo why am I giving it non-recommendation? The problem is two-fold.\n\nFirst problem is the game itself. It's a continuation of the paths Conviction paved: but this time I think it lost its identity in the process. It has Splinter Cell in the title, but honestly, it's more like a generic action game that's wearing its skin and masquerading as a Sam Fisher game. It's almost like it even tries to be a \"Ubisoft All-Stars\" game, because it often resembles Assassin's Creed more than Splinter Cell, and at one point even has a goddamn 1st person shooter part. Also, none of the characters resemble themselves, the least being Fisher himself. I think the new actor was miscast, because this Fisher sounds like he's in his 30s when he should be at least 20 years older. As for the game itself: while Conviction was too short, Blacklist on the other hand overstays its welcome. The gameplay gets so repetitive without the story really moving that much forward at the same time. Then you just want to get it over with, getting more bored with every new 100th kill you do because the drone enemy waves are just unceasing.\n\nThe other problem has to do with Ubisoft being so adamant about forcing their Uplay / Ubisoft Connect DRM on you. I would be fine with it, if it didn't cause problems in the background, but unfortunately it does. It really, really does. At first, the game would crash every 25 minutes or so. This was fixed when I launched the game directly from Ubisoft Connect (that's also why it only shows 1.3 hrs played): but that's not how I want to play the games I purchase on STEAM. But then there's also all the content that's inaccessible, whether it's by shut down servers or the fact that the DRM (Uplay) is outdated. The mission map displays missions that you simply can not access. Finally, the DRM had one more infuriating bug in which the ending cutscene will not play properly, like with Double Agent before. The player HAS to know to switch to windowed mode right when the final battle starts if they want to see the ending, which is bullshit.\n\nSo, in a nutshell: \n- story that gets boring and the gameplay is so repetitive and has you mow down hundreds of enemies\n- the characters appear to be mere doppelgangers of their past selves; a Splinter Cell game this is not\n- content that in reality is unaccessible \n- crashes and other sorts of bugs that you manually need to figure out, thanks to the Ubisoft Connect extra DRM\n\nConviction was great but this was not a worthy successor to that. If Conviction is Matrix, then this is Matrix Resurrections. Too bad that the franchise ended with such mediocrity.",
